Which is interesting work, by the way.

267
00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:16.590
Ew.

268
00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.010
It sounds a little gross.

269
00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:21.510
Well, it was a psychological office.

270
00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:28.170
So what these psychologists did was if you were applying for an executive position at an

271
00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.830
important company, you had to go through a psychological evaluation.

272
00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:36.590
And then these psychologists would determine whether or not they could recommend you for the

273
00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:37.270
job or not.

274
00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:38.690
And you wrote it all down?

275
00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:44.550
I transcribed all of the sessions, all of the therapy sessions with the executives.

276
00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.690
Jenna, this sounds like a HBO series.

277
00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:47.810
Oh, my gosh.

278
00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:48.810
You should pitch that.

279
00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:49.210
Okay.

280
00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:49.490
Sorry.

281
00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:50.250
We're getting off topic.

282
00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:55.770
Well, I want to say in my time doing it, there was only one person who was not recommended.

283
00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:59.030
And it was because they felt he had some violent tendencies.

284
00:11:59.330 --> 00:11:59.550
Okay.

285
00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:00.770
Well, this is taking a turn.

286
00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.850
But can you imagine how suspenseful it was for me to be typing out that information?

287
00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:06.230
I was like, what are they?

288
00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:08.290
25-year-old Jenna was like, ooh.

289
00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:09.170
Yeah.

290
00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:10.690
Well, that's what I was doing when I was 25.

291
00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.850
When BJ Novak was 25 years old, he was writing this television show.

292
00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:17.070
And this was his first job writing for a television show.

293
00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:17.410
I know.

294
00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:17.710
All of it.

295
00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:20.290
And his first script was Diversity Day.

296
00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:22.110
What the hell is going on?

297
00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.930
BJ, just settle down on being such an achiever, okay?

298
00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:25.810
Can you just relax?

299
00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:31.370
I had to reach out to BJ because I wanted to ask him, BJ, what was it like?

300
00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:37.270
Because here we had the British version of the show that was so acclaimed, and you are

301
00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:41.350
now tasked with writing the first original episode.

302
00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:42.730
The pilot after the pilot.

303
00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:44.030
The pilot after the pilot.

304
00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:49.270
He explained the original writing staff for The Office was Greg Daniels, the executive

305
00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:55.370
producer showrunner, Paul Lieberstein, Mindy Kaling, Mike Schur, and BJ Novak.

306
00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:56.870
What a room, by the way.

307
00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:57.810
What a room.

308
00:12:57.890 --> 00:12:58.250
I know.

309
00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:01.510
And then they had Larry Wilmore and Lester Lewis, who were consulting.

310
00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:08.350
This room full of writers broke down the plots and storylines of the five remaining episodes

311
00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:09.470
of season one.

312
00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:11.670
And they did this as a group.

313
00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.350
So they created outlines for each of these episodes.

314
00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:19.150
And then a different writer got assigned a different outline.

315
00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.210
And he got assigned the Diversity Day episode.

316
00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:21.450
Okay.

317
00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:22.130
So wait.

318
00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:23.130
I just want to make sure I understand.

319
00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:27.070
So they would take storylines and outlines and break them down as a group.

320
00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:27.530
Yes.

321
00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.330
And then you would get assigned which storyline was yours.

322
00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:30.650
Yes.

323
00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.090
And then you had to fill in the dialogue and different story points.

324
00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:37.910
And you do a good amount of writing on your episode.

325
00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.190
But the idea for the episode comes from the group collective.

326
00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:41.770
From the room.

327
00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:42.070
Okay.

328
00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:42.390
Right.

329
00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:47.570
And also at that time, they didn't know what order they were going to air.

330
00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:50.210
So he did not have the pressure.

331
00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:50.910
Yes.

332
00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:52.330
Because that's what I was thinking.

333
00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:53.190
Of writing the first one.

334
00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:53.610
Yeah.

335
00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.430
That would be so much pressure.

336
00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:56.570
I would freeze up.

337
00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:57.670
And I remember that.

338
00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:02.190
I remember that when we came back after getting picked up and we were going to shoot these

339
00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.510
five additional episodes, we had five completed scripts.

340
00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.170
And then they decided which one they were going to shoot.

341
00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.450
They didn't come out weekly.

342
00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.970
They did it collectively before we started the season.

343
00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:12.570
Mm-hmm.

344
00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.870
Which, I guess that takes some of the pressure off.

345
00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:16.970
It would.

346
00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:17.490
Yeah.

347
00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.470
And I have another fast fact.

348
00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:19.830
All right.

349
00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:24.290
You do your fast facts because I've got some delicious note cards waiting for you.

350
00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:24.750
All right.

351
00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.450
Some Kinsey tidbits over here.

352
00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.730
I enjoy digesting your note cards.

353
00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:29.390
Oh, okay.

354
00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:31.130
Digest this first, though.

355
00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:31.630
What?

356
00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:35.730
People, a lot of people want to know, where did the idea for this episode come from?

357
00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:41.230
This episode is based on the real-life experience of our writer's assistant, Tom.

358
00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.410
So when they were in that writer's room, they throw out ideas for episodes.

359
00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:49.970
And Tom told this story about how when he was in college, he took a class.

360
00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:55.010
And in the class, they did this exercise where they had to put note cards on their heads

361
00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.670
that said different ethnicities.

362
00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.390
And then they had to go around and regard one another as that ethnicity.

363
00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:01.490
Where did he go to school?

364
00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:02.110
Using stereotypes.

365
00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:05.560
Where did he?

366
00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:06.120
Don't know.

367
00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:06.940
Holy moly.

368
00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:08.440
But that happened.

369
00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:09.100
Okay.

370
00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.660
So let that sink in.

371
00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:11.160
Yeah.

372
00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:12.940
That happened to people.

373
00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:15.260
Well, here's the thing I feel like about the show.

374
00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.520
And listen, you guys watch it.

375
00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:16.920
You know.

376
00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.280
There's a lot that happens that you're like, oh, yeah, something like that kind of happened

377
00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:20.780
at my job.

378
00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:21.360
Yeah.

379
00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:28.180
I mean, when I worked at 1-800-DENTIS, we had to go to these human resources meetings

380
00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:31.340
and they would act out skits for us about inappropriate behavior.

381
00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:32.780
Well, you know what?

382
00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:37.360
Remember, we used to have to sit through those sexual harassment seminars on the office.

383
00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:37.580
Yeah.

384
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.180
The whole cast and crew would gather in the warehouse.

385
00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.260
And once a year, we had to get-

386
00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:43.400
And the ABC made us do it.

387
00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:43.740
Yeah.

388
00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.400
Made us do sexual harassment training.

389
00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.820
And then we signed papers saying we'd all behave appropriately at work.

390
00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.960
But do you remember what happened like in season four or five?

391
00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:54.900
The video package that they showed us-

392
00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:55.820
Was our show.

393
00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:56.100
Was-

394
00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:56.800
Of what not to do.

395
00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:57.220
Yes.

396
00:15:57.320 --> 00:15:57.520
Yeah.

397
00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:58.880
Clips from the office.

398
00:15:59.260 --> 00:15:59.400
Yeah.

399
00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.800
We were sitting there watching clips of our show as examples of ways not to behave in

400
00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:04.200
a workplace.

401
00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.340
I've had actually a lot of people tell me that, that it comes up in different ways

402
00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:11.600
that they're in their- either college or work that our show is like, what not to do.

403
00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:12.600
All right.

404
00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:14.280
I've got one more fast fact.

