524
00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:22.780
Hi, Heather.

525
00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:24.200
Heather asked,

526
00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:26.560
since it was only the second episode,

527
00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:28.940
what was the atmosphere on set like filming?

528
00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:31.760
And I thought that was a really good question

529
00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:37.400
because we shot Diversity Day about six months after we shot the pilot.

530
00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:41.000
We took this really long break because we were waiting to find out

531
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:42.840
if we were going to get picked up or not.

532
00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:48.800
And it was so thrilling to be reunited again.

533
00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:52.020
I remember when we were watching Diversity Day, people have asked me,

534
00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:53.340
when did you feel like you had a hit?

535
00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:56.260
And I said, it's the moment we were all in the room,

536
00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.060
and we all had those note cards taped to our foreheads.

537
00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:02.320
And I was just looking around at all of us,

538
00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:05.300
and Steve was being Michael, and he was being so hilarious.

539
00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:09.840
And I thought, oh, my God, if we can just get anyone to watch the show,

540
00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:10.600
we're going to be a hit.

541
00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.940
If anyone sees it, it's so good.

542
00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:18.400
And it really was in that moment, I was like, oh, wow, this is lightning in a bottle.

543
00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.600
Now, how do we get the world to watch it?

544
00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.580
Aw, I love that, Ange.

545
00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:24.500
Listen, should we take a break?

546
00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:25.860
Yeah, let's take a break.

547
00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:27.700
I have lots of note cards.

548
00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:28.160
Well, perfect.

549
00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:30.540
When we come back, we will really break down Diversity Day

550
00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.840
and get into the scenes and tell you all our stories.

551
00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:34.440
You say diversity.

552
00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.160
I say diversity.

553
00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:37.180
What?

554
00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:38.540
Yeah, it's just been bugging me.

555
00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:38.920
But anyway.

556
00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:40.920
Okay, let's go to a break.

557
00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:51.920
And we're back.

558
00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:52.500
Hello.

559
00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:57.660
All right, so the Diversity Day episode starts with Michael explaining that today

560
00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:00.880
the office is having a mandatory diversity training.

561
00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:01.480
Yes.

562
00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:06.320
We also learned that today is the day that Jim is going to make his biggest sale of the year.

563
00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:09.000
He explains he only has to make one phone call,

564
00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:11.920
and it amounts to 25% of his total sales for the year.

565
00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.120
But Dwight keeps making it impossible for him to make that call.

566
00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:20.340
And so the first way that Dwight makes it impossible for him to make this phone call

567
00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:23.500
is that he must shred paper at his desk.

568
00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:24.580
Oh, Dwight.

569
00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:27.100
Which I always thought was funny because, you know,

570
00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:30.360
Pam has that industrial-sized shredder behind her desk.

571
00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.460
Pam has a shredder like the size of a Xerox machine behind her desk.

572
00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:35.840
Which we never use.

573
00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:36.320
Never use.

574
00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:36.600
Ever.

575
00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.460
I never shredded a single thing in it.

576
00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.500
And it used to get in the way when the camera—

577
00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:42.180
You had to move it.

578
00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:46.100
And it was a big argument over whether or not they could move it.

579
00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.400
Like, the integrity of the documentary crew,

580
00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:52.400
would they have moved the industrial shredder or not?

581
00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:55.160
And so I remember a day we were on set,

582
00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:57.500
and they wanted to get this shot behind my desk.

583
00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.960
And they said, if we could just move the shredder,

584
00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:03.540
we could get the shot you're asking for.

585
00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:08.860
And it was like a 30-minute argument over whether or not they could move or not move the shredder.

586
00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:09.120
Right.

587
00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:12.220
Because they didn't want to, like, ruin the integrity of the documentary look.

588
00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:12.290
Yes.

589
00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:13.940
This was a big thing all the time.

590
00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:17.380
They decided, you know, you can't move walls.

591
00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:18.220
You can't move windows.

592
00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:19.660
You can't move furniture.

593
00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:24.260
They did agree you could move this one plant around, the ficus.

594
00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:25.440
You could move the ficus.

595
00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:29.440
They clearly agreed that you could move that conference table out and put chairs,

596
00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.380
and then all of a sudden the conference table would be back.

597
00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:32.240
I've had—

598
00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.100
Where did the conference table go?

599
00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:34.020
Where did the conference table go?

600
00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:34.700
I know.

601
00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:38.400
I've had so many people write to me, like, on social media be like,

602
00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:40.040
what happened with the conference table?

603
00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:41.220
It was there, and then it's gone.

604
00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:42.600
Where does the conference table go?

605
00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:46.660
And I told them, I said, you know what I would have loved at the end of one episode?

606
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:52.380
All it would take is one episode, and the whole nine seasons, if at the end credits,

607
00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:58.560
it just showed, like, Kevin and, like, Jim breaking down the conference table and Dwight

608
00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:02.540
and, like, stacking it in some storage thing and then having to go get it and put it back together.

609
00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:04.100
But just as the credits rolled.

610
00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:04.660
Yes.

611
00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:08.800
I would love to see them carrying the conference table down to the warehouse or something.

612
00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:08.970
Like, in pieces.

613
00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:10.000
Because where is it?

614
00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:10.700
I don't know.

615
00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:12.240
There is nowhere for it to be.

616
00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:12.860
I don't know.

617
00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:13.720
It is.

618
00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:15.840
That is one of the great mysteries of the show.

619
00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:17.020
Where is the conference table?

620
00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:20.300
Every time Michael was, like, conference room meeting, that they were like, ugh,

621
00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:22.100
because they knew they had to break down that table.

622
00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:23.960
Okay, okay.

623
00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:27.040
Well, do you remember that shredding scene where Rain is shredding the paper?

624
00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:28.140
Yes.

625
00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:29.560
And so he's shredding paper.

626
00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:30.280
He's shredding paper.

627
00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:36.820
So then Rain notices that there's a special slot on his personal shredder for a credit card,

628
00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.100
and he kind of stopped the filming.

629
00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:42.280
And he said, guys, I think I should shred a credit card.

630
00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:44.960
And we were thinking, well, okay.

631
00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.260
And he said, no, there's a special slot for it.

632
00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:48.580
I know.

633
00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:49.960
And everyone was like, is it going to work?

634
00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:52.280
And so he did it.

635
00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:52.900
He did it.

636
00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:56.620
They got him, like, a fake, like, credit card kind of thing, the props department.

637
00:25:56.840 --> 00:25:59.140
And he did it, but it almost broke that shredder.

638
00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.380
It started going like, eee.

639
00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:06.580
I remember sitting there and thinking, is it, you know, is it, what's happening?

640
00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:07.580
What's happening?

641
00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.000
But he was so determined.

642
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.920
I also think Rain is like a gadget person a little bit.

643
00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:13.360
So he was like, oh, there's a thing on here.

644
00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:14.160
I need to do it.

645
00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:15.960
Yeah, I need to work this part of the machine.

646
00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:16.640
I need to do it.

647
00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:18.380
Angela, would you like to do a card?

648
00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:19.680
I would, Jenna Fisher.

649
00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:23.480
Okay, so this card applies to the opening shot of the whole episode.

650
00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.960
There is a great establishing shot of the Homer Simpson doll.

651
00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:30.800
The camera is going past Phyllis's desk, and it's sitting right there.

652
00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:36.220
And, you know, it just makes me smile because I know that Greg Daniels wrote on The Simpsons,

653
00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:41.500
and this was his little, like, nod to, like, the first part of his career, right?

654
00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:41.960
Yes.

655
00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.020
And it sat there all nine years.

656
00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:44.520
It did.

657
00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:45.300
That Simpson doll.

658
00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:46.560
So I love that.

659
00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.340
That's just, like, kind of a dorky moment.

660
00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:50.400
You guys know I love the things in the background.

661
00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:53.360
Well, you know what he did for the main caSt.

662
00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:54.000
I'm sorry.

663
00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:55.380
I do know this.

664
00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:56.140
You do know.

665
00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:57.960
Are you saying I'm sorry because I didn't get one?

666
00:26:58.060 --> 00:26:58.340
Yeah.

667
00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.100
Because you're calling me not main cast?

668
00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:02.940
We called you supporting caSt.

669
00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:03.900
Is this?

670
00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:07.540
As a gift.

671
00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:11.620
You guys, the rest of us didn't become series regular until Booze Cruise.

672
00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:12.840
That's a story for later.

673
00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:14.820
But in the beginning, the main—

674
00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:16.040
There were just five of us.

675
00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:20.940
Well, there weren't just the five of you, but you were the five that were the, quote, series regulars.

676
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:21.480
That's right.

677
00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:24.220
There were just the five series regulars, I should say.

678
00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:27.160
And the rest of us were just trash in the corner.

679
00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:32.260
No, but we had to do a lot of extra stuff.

680
00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:33.900
You had to do press and whatnot.

681
00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:40.560
We had to do press, and we had to do these crazy photo shoots, and we did all this stuff as the show was launching.

682
00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:42.860
Listen, there is no debating.

683
00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:46.360
You guys were the core main cast, and the rest of us filled out the picture.

684
00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:50.900
And then it grew, and then the cast grew, and then we had all of these series regulars.

685
00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:58.760
But I'm going to give you a little shiitake sometimes.

686
00:27:59.160 --> 00:27:59.800
All right, listen.

687
00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:11.040
So as a gift, Greg had the illustrators over at The Simpsons turn us all into Simpson characters.

688
00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:11.540
Mm-hmm.

689
00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:18.900
And it was me, John, Rain, and Steve huddled around a computer.

690
00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:21.700
Now that I'm saying it, I realize I think B.J.

691
00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.300
Novak was left out of this photo.

692
00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:24.120
Sorry, B.J.

693
00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:32.220
But we were Simpsonized, and he gave us these illustrations in like a frame.

694
00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:33.800
It's really cool.

695
00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:34.640
It's really cool.

696
00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:35.700
It's in my living room.

697
00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:38.060
It's like a treasured item from the show.

698
00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:39.100
Jenna is a Simpson.

699
00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.460
I have a Simpson version of me.

700
00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:42.340
All right.

701
00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:47.600
I want to talk about Pam's free sell, and so does BNG Chris on Twitter.

702
00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:49.720
Well, let's hear it, BNG Chris.

703
00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:50.500
All right.

704
00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:53.640
He asked if we were actually good at free sell.

705
00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:57.440
And the answer is, oh, yes, we were.

706
00:28:57.600 --> 00:28:58.700
You want to know why?

707
00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:00.380
This is one of my note cards, too.

708
00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:04.260
We didn't get Wi-Fi until July of 2006.

709
00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:10.360
And it was such a big deal on our set that a memo went around, and I saved the memo.

710
00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:15.920
It was because, to give you some context, we shot this episode in 2004.

711
00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:16.800
Yes.

712
00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:19.960
And so it wasn't until 2006 that we got internet.

713
00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.020
So for the pilot, they were nothing.

714
00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.040
They were literally like pieces of plastic.

715
00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:25.480
I have to tell you something, Angela.

716
00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:25.740
What?

717
00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:27.900
My computer on the pilot was real.

718
00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:29.580
Son of a b****, Jenna.

719
00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:30.100
I'm so sorry.

720
00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:31.880
Maincast got real computers.

721
00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:32.720
Shut up!

722
00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.420
You and John and Rain had actual computers?

723
00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:39.240
Yeah.

724
00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.880
No internet, but you had games for the pilot?

725
00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:42.220
Yeah.

726
00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:44.120
Will this ever end?

727
00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:45.880
Will it ever end?

728
00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:47.220
All these things I'm learning.

729
00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:52.760
Well, back in accounting, guys, we had a piece of plastic that they made look like a computer.

730
00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:54.640
You just had to stare at a plastic box.

731
00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:57.200
Piece of plastic that did nothing, so we passed notes.

732
00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.260
We passed a bunch of notes.

733
00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:01.200
I haven't known when to tell you this because you've told the story before that our computers…

734
00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:02.360
And you have sat silent?

735
00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:06.340
I haven't known how to tell you that all along I had a real computer.

736
00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:07.380
Oh, my God.

737
00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:09.360
What else is going to come out in this podcast?

738
00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:10.140
I don't know.

739
00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:11.380
Holy crap.

740
00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:14.680
Well, we passed a lot of notes, and I saved those too.

741
00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:15.620
Oscar can draw.

742
00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:21.900
He's a really good drawer, and he would draw me and Brian as Kevin and Angela, and I saved them all.

743
00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:22.940
So there, Jenna…

744
00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:25.960
That's what you were doing while I was on my actual computer.

745
00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:26.780
Well, whatever.

746
00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:32.540
The answer to that story is yes, we were very good at solitaire because it's all we had to do for like two years.

747
00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:33.280
It's true.

748
00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:38.260
And we had on-set free-sell competitions, and we would compete with the cast and crew.

749
00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:41.920
In between scenes, the crew would sit down at a desk, and they would play free-sell.

750
00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:45.160
And Kate Flannery held the title for a very long time.

751
00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:46.680
She was really very good.

752
00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:47.260
She is a master.

753
00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:48.580
Phyllis was also very good.

754
00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:50.380
Kate is so good, though.

755
00:30:50.660 --> 00:30:50.900
Yes.

756
00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:54.980
With the free-sell, the competition was who could do it in the fewest moves.

757
00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:56.740
And yeah.

758
00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.100
Kate was really good.

759
00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:00.100
I never made the top 10.

760
00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:01.760
I would also lose patience.

761
00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:04.760
Okay.

762
00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:05.260
All right.

763
00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:06.080
Get going.

764
00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:07.540
What else you got over there, Fisher?

765
00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.800
What else are you hiding from me that you're not telling me?

766
00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:12.480
What other perks did you have?

767
00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.300
Well, this wasn't really a perk, but this was a little sneaky.

768
00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:22.760
So, during this episode, off-camera, there was a whole thing going on, and it involved

769
00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:23.580
me and Phyllis.

770
00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:31.420
So, Phyllis and I are from St. Louis, Missouri, and during this time, the Cardinals were in

771
00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:32.060
the playoffs.

772
00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:33.140
I remember this.

773
00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:39.000
They ended up in the World Series against the Boston Red Sox, and in between scenes, Phyllis

774
00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:42.780
and I would sneak back to my trailer and watch the Cardinals games.

775
00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:43.460
Yes.

776
00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:47.160
And we were rooting hard for the Cardinals, and it was very difficult for us because

